<div class="credit-copy">
      <p>Night lights: <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/earth-at-night/maps/">NASA Earth Observatory</a> / Joshua Stevens, Suomi NPP VIIRS. Black Marble 2016, 500 m imagery streamed as grayscale light tiles; color and exposure adjusted.</p>
      <p>Surface: <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/blue-marble-next-generation/base-map/">NASA Blue Marble Next Generation</a>, October 2004, 500 m imagery. Reto Stöckli and the NASA Earth Observatory team.</p>
      <p>Terrain: <a href="https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/products/etopo-global-relief-model">NOAA NCEI ETOPO 2022</a>, ice-surface elevations. Ocean mask: <a href="https://www.naturalearthdata.com/">Natural Earth</a> 1:10m land polygons. Heights resampled for rendering; not live terrain or sea state.</p>
      <p>Detailed terrain: <a href="https://copernicus-dem-30m.s3.amazonaws.com/readme.html">Copernicus GLO-90</a>, streamed for nearby ground. Produced using Copernicus WorldDEM-90 © DLR e.V. 2010-2014 and © Airbus Defence and Space GmbH 2014-2018 provided under COPERNICUS by the European Union and ESA; all rights reserved.</p>
      <p>Clouds: <a href="https://visibleearth.nasa.gov/images/57747/blue-marble-clouds">NASA Blue Marble</a> / Reto Stöckli. Composite imagery from 2001, resized and used as coverage for an illustrative cloud volume; not live weather or measured cloud heights.</p>
      <p>Orbit: <a href="https://celestrak.org/NORAD/elements/gp.php?CATNR=25544&amp;FORMAT=JSON">CelesTrak ISS elements</a>, saved 6 October 2026. The replay opens shortly before moonrise and uses <a href="https://github.com/shashwatak/satellite-js">satellite.js</a> (MIT). Orbital elements are a saved snapshot, not a live tracking feed.</p>
      <p>The Moon's illuminated phase follows today's date on your device. Its position and the rest of the sky follow the saved orbital replay.</p>
      <p>Stars: <a href="https://github.com/astronexus/HYG-Database">HYG v4.1 / David Nash</a>, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>. Magnitude-limited catalog converted to a compact binary subset; positions advanced from J2000.</p>
      <p>Milky Way: <a href="https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/4851/">NASA/Goddard Space Flight Center Scientific Visualization Studio</a>. Gaia DR2: ESA/Gaia/DPAC. Catalog-based sky map converted to AVIF and rendered with adjusted contrast and exposure.</p>
      <p>Moon, planets and celestial coordinates: <a href="https://github.com/cosinekitty/astronomy">Astronomy Engine / Don Cross</a> (MIT). Synthetic lens PSF; illustrative exposure.</p>
